# Smallstep
## 1. Install step cli (one machine 1)
```bash
# Ubuntu / Debian
wget https://dl.smallstep.com/gh-release/cli/gh-release-header/v0.30.0/step-cli_0.30.0-1_amd64.deb
sudo dpkg -i step-cli_0.30.0-1_amd64.deb

# Amazon Linux / RHEL
wget https://dl.smallstep.com/gh-release/cli/gh-release-header/v0.30.0/step-cli_0.30.0-1_amd64.rpm
sudo rpm -i step-cli_0.30.0-1_amd64.rpm

# macOS
brew install step

step version
```

## 2. Create Root CA (valid 10 yrs) - use once, then keep root_ca.key offline
```bash
step certificate create "team03.io Root CA" root_ca.crt root_ca.key \
  --profile root-ca --not-after 87600h --no-password --insecure
```

## 3. Create Intermedidate CA, singed by root (valid 5 yrs)
```bash
step certificate create "team03.io Intermediate CA" intermediate_ca.crt intermediate_ca.key \
  --profile intermediate-ca --ca root_ca.crt --ca-key root_ca.key --not-after 43800h --no-password --insecure

# from now on ONLY the intermediate signs certs. root_ca.key is not needed any more -> move it away / keep offline
```

## 4. Create the 2 leaf certed, signed by Intermediate CA (valid 1 yr)
```bash
step certificate create dashboard.team03.io dashboard.crt dashboard.key \
  --profile leaf --ca intermediate_ca.crt --ca-key intermediate_ca.key --not-after 8760h --no-password --insecure

step certificate create counting.team03.io counting.crt counting.key \
  --profile leaf --ca intermediate_ca.crt --ca-key intermediate_ca.key --not-after 8760h --no-password --insecure
```

## 5. Build the chain file + check
```bash
cat intermediate_ca.crt root_ca.crt > chain.pem            # order: intermediate first, then root

step certificate inspect dashboard.crt --short
openssl verify -CAfile root_ca.crt -untrusted intermediate_ca.crt dashboard.crt    # -> dashboard.crt: OK
openssl verify -CAfile root_ca.crt -untrusted intermediate_ca.crt counting.crt     # -> counting.crt: OK
```

## 6. Files you have now -> where they go
```bash
root_ca.crt          ->  laptop browser trust store, dashboard EC2 trust store   (only the ROOT is trusted)
root_ca.key          ->  offline / secret, not used again until the intermediate expires
intermediate_ca.crt  ->  part of chain.pem
intermediate_ca.key  ->  stays on jumphost (secret), signs new leaf certs
chain.pem            ->  ACM "Certificate chain" field   (intermediate + root)
dashboard.crt/key    ->  ACM import  ->  dashboard ALB 443 listener
counting.crt/key     ->  ACM import  ->  counting ALB 443 listener
```

## Checking: check cert content
```bash
openssl x509 -in certificate.crt -text -noout

step certificate inspect chain.pem
```

Why 3 levels?   root (trust anchor, offline)  ->  intermediate (does the signing)  ->  leaf (the server cert)
- If intermediate_ca.key leaks: make a new intermediate from the root, re-issue leafs. Browsers keep trusting the same root.
- If root_ca.key leaks: everything must be redone and the root re-imported in every browser / VM. So keep it offline.
- Browser / VM only need root_ca.crt. The ALB sends leaf + intermediate (that is what chain.pem in ACM is for).

Notes
- --no-password --insecure  = private key not encrypted. Fine for a lab, ACM needs it unencrypted anyway.
- Renew: before 1 year, re-run step 4 and re-import into ACM (ACM does not renew imported certs).
- step-ca server, provisioners, ACME, auto-renew, systemd = advanced, NOT needed for this lab.

